\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

We frame stagnation detection as window-level binary classification over agent trajectories.
Silver labels are generated via weak supervision from three labeling functions, then used to train and evaluate three detection approaches: structural features, a neural embedding probe, and an LLM-as-judge baseline.
Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} provides a system overview.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_pipeline.pdf}
\caption{System overview. (Left) Agent trajectories are segmented into overlapping windows of $k{=}5$ steps. (Center) Three labeling functions vote on each window; majority vote produces silver labels with confidence 1--3. (Right) Three detection approaches compared: structural features (30-dim), NL embedding probe (GRU), and LLM-as-judge (eval only, not trained on silver labels).}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure*}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Problem Formulation}
\label{sec:problem}

Given an agent trajectory $T = (a_1, a_2, \ldots, a_n)$ where each $a_t$ is an action (e.g., a shell command, file edit, or API call), we partition it into overlapping sliding windows $w_i = (a_i, \ldots, a_{i+k-1})$ with window size $k{=}5$ and stride 1.
The task is to assign each window a binary label $y_i \in \{0, 1\}$, where 0 denotes \emph{stagnation} (the agent is stuck or unproductive) and 1 denotes \emph{progress} (the agent is making meaningful headway toward the task goal).

The window formulation offers two advantages over alternatives.
Compared to step-level scoring, windows capture short-range temporal patterns---such as a sequence of repeated failed attempts---that individual actions cannot express.
Compared to trajectory-level labeling, windows provide the granularity needed to pinpoint \emph{when} stagnation begins and ends within a single task attempt, enabling downstream interventions such as early termination or strategy switching.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Weak Supervision Pipeline}
\label{sec:weak_supervision}

No gold-standard annotations exist for agent stagnation at the window level.
Manual annotation is expensive: each window requires reviewing $k$ actions with full context (command text, outputs, file diffs), taking an estimated 2--3 minutes per window.
We therefore adopt a data programming approach \citep{ratner2016dataprogramming,ratner2017snorkel}: multiple labeling functions (LFs) provide noisy votes that are aggregated into silver labels.
Each LF $\lambda_j : w_i \to \{0, 1, \bot\}$ examines a window and emits a vote: stagnation (0), progress (1), or abstain ($\bot$).

\subsubsection{Labeling Functions}
\label{sec:labeling_functions}

We design three LFs that target complementary signals of stagnation (Table~\ref{tab:lf_design}).

\paragraph{Command repetition ($\lambda_1$).}
Computes the proportion of repeated commands within window $w_i$.
A high repetition ratio triggers a stagnation vote, capturing overt repetitive behavior such as re-running failing commands.
This LF never abstains: it votes stagnation when the repetition ratio exceeds a threshold and progress otherwise.
It votes stagnation on 65.5\% of SWE-Agent windows and 92.2\% of OpenHands windows; the elevated OH rate reflects differences in action distributions across agent architectures (\S\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).

\paragraph{File diff delta ($\lambda_2$).}
Measures the fraction of steps within $w_i$ that produce code modifications (file creates, edits, or deletes).
A low modification frequency triggers a stagnation vote; windows with more than one edited file receive a progress vote; windows with exactly one or zero edits abstain ($\bot$).
On OpenHands, this LF achieves 87\% correctness on progress windows, making it the most reliable cross-agent signal.

\paragraph{Outcome backpropagation ($\lambda_3$).}
Uses the final task outcome (resolved/unresolved) as distant supervision.
For unresolved tasks, the last two windows and any windows following a timeout or stuck signal receive stagnation votes; resolved tasks receive progress votes in the corresponding windows.
All other windows abstain ($\bot$).
Unlike $\lambda_1$ and $\lambda_2$, which operate on local window content, $\lambda_3$ propagates a global signal backward through time.
This makes it a form of distant supervision: individually noisy, but complementary to the real-time signals when aggregated.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
\textbf{LF} & \textbf{Signal} & \textbf{SWE Stag\,\%} & \textbf{OH Stag\,\%} \\
\midrule
$\lambda_1$ (cmd\_rep)    & Repetition & 65.5 & 92.2 \\
$\lambda_2$ (file\_diff)  & Output     & 57.3 & 42.1 \\
$\lambda_3$ (outcome\_bp) & Outcome    & 23.9 & 19.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Labeling function design. Stag\,\% = fraction of windows receiving a stagnation vote from the LF (remaining windows receive progress or abstain). SWE = SWE-Agent; OH = OpenHands.}
\label{tab:lf_design}
\end{table}

\subsubsection{Label Aggregation}
\label{sec:label_aggregation}

Silver labels are produced by majority vote among non-abstaining LFs:
\begin{equation}
y_i = \text{MV}\bigl(\{\lambda_j(w_i) : \lambda_j(w_i) \neq \bot\}\bigr)
\label{eq:mv}
\end{equation}
We define the confidence of each label as the number of non-abstaining LFs that agree with the winning label: $\text{conf}_i = |\{j : \lambda_j(w_i) \neq \bot \;\text{and}\; \lambda_j(w_i) = y_i\}| \in \{1, 2, 3\}$.
Conf${=}1$ arises when a single non-abstaining LF determines the label---either because only one LF votes or because two LFs disagree with no clear majority---producing the most ambiguous labels.
The conf${\geq}$2 subset, where at least two non-abstaining LFs agree on the winning label, serves as the primary evaluation set throughout this work.
This stratification allows us to separately analyze model behavior on high-confidence vs.\ ambiguous windows (\S\ref{sec:main_results}).

%-------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Detection Methods}
\label{sec:detection_methods}

\subsubsection{Structural Features}
\label{sec:structural_features}

We extract a 30-dimensional feature vector from each window, requiring no pretrained model or GPU.
Features span four categories:
(1)~\emph{repetition}: exact command repeat ratio, fuzzy match ratio (Levenshtein-based), and unique command ratio;
(2)~\emph{file operations}: counts of file creates, modifications, and deletions within the window;
(3)~\emph{edit patterns}: edit distances between consecutive actions, capturing how much the agent's behavior changes step-to-step;
(4)~\emph{output statistics}: entropy of command outputs and action type distribution features.
These features are fed to three classifiers: Logistic Regression (LR), Random Forest (RF), and Gradient Boosting (GB).
Because several structural features overlap with the signals used by the labeling functions (e.g., repetition ratios relate to $\lambda_1$), structural classifiers can partially reconstruct the LF voting process.
We discuss this circularity and its implications in \S\ref{sec:main_results}.

\subsubsection{NL Embedding Probe (ProgressProbe)}
\label{sec:progressprobe}

Each action $a_t$ is encoded as a natural language description and embedded using Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B \citep{zhang2025qwen3embedding} into a 1024-dimensional vector.
The $k$ embeddings within a window form a sequence that is processed by a 2-layer GRU \citep{cho2014gru} with hidden size 256 and dropout 0.1.
An attention mechanism \citep{bahdanau2015attention} computes a weighted combination of the GRU hidden states, yielding a single window-level representation.
This representation is passed through a fully connected layer with sigmoid activation for binary classification.
Training uses binary cross-entropy loss with early stopping (patience 5) on a validation set.

The GRU architecture is motivated by the sequential nature of actions within a window: the order in which commands are issued (e.g., repeated failures followed by a different approach) carries diagnostic information that bag-of-embeddings methods discard.
We also evaluate two simpler embedding baselines to isolate the contribution of temporal modeling: (1)~mean-pooled Qwen3 embeddings with logistic regression, and (2)~cosine similarity between consecutive window embeddings with a validation-tuned threshold.

\subsubsection{LLM-as-Judge Baseline}
\label{sec:llm_judge}

We prompt Claude Sonnet 4.6 in zero-shot mode to classify each window as stagnation or progress, given the textual descriptions of actions within the window.
This baseline serves exclusively for evaluation---it is not used in the silver label pipeline and incurs \$11.66 in API cost for the SWE-Agent test set alone, making it impractical for large-scale labeling.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Temporal Label Models}
\label{sec:temporal_models}

The three LFs occupy distinct temporal niches relative to stagnation onset (\S\ref{sec:lf_temporal}): $\lambda_1$ leads by ${\sim}2$ steps, $\lambda_2$ is near-concurrent, and $\lambda_3$ lags by ${\sim}7$ steps.
This motivates exploring whether encoding temporal structure into label aggregation can improve over majority vote.

\subsubsection{Time-Varying Weights (TWV)}
\label{sec:twv}

TWV applies Gaussian kernel smoothing to each LF's activation sequence:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\lambda}_j(t) = \sum_{t'} K_\sigma(t - t') \cdot \lambda_j(t')
\label{eq:twv}
\end{equation}
where $K_\sigma$ is a Gaussian kernel with bandwidth $\sigma$.
Smoothed activations replace raw votes for conf${=}1$ windows (where exactly one LF disagrees), potentially flipping labels in temporally ambiguous regions.
The bandwidth $\sigma$ controls the extent of temporal context: larger values incorporate more distant steps.

\subsubsection{Hidden Markov Models}
\label{sec:hmm}

We model the label sequence as a Hidden Markov Model \citep{safranchik2020linkedhmm} with two hidden states (stagnation, progress) and observations defined by the joint activation pattern of the three LFs.
Transition and emission parameters are estimated via Expectation-Maximization (EM).
The HMM captures temporal persistence---stagnation episodes tend to span multiple consecutive windows---which majority vote, operating independently per window, cannot model.

\subsubsection{Lag-Aware HMM}
\label{sec:lag_hmm}

The standard HMM treats all LFs as synchronous.
Lag-Aware HMM extends this by explicitly modeling each LF's temporal offset: $\lambda_1$ is shifted by $-2$ steps, $\lambda_2$ by $-1$, and $\lambda_3$ by $+7$, estimated from the temporal dynamics analysis (\S\ref{sec:lf_temporal}).
To prevent divergence, we apply two regularization strategies: (1)~\emph{confidence anchoring}, which fixes labels for conf${\geq}$2 windows and restricts EM updates to conf${=}1$ windows; and (2)~a prior strength of 5000 on transition parameters.
These constraints keep the model close to the majority vote baseline while allowing it to resolve temporally ambiguous labels.
The intuition is that aligning each LF's activation to its natural temporal role should produce cleaner emission estimates and, consequently, more accurate Viterbi decodings on ambiguous windows.

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate stagnation detection across four axes: in-domain accuracy, cross-agent transfer, robustness to design choices, and temporal dynamics of labeling functions.
All reported metrics are evaluated against silver labels derived from majority vote over three labeling functions (\S\ref{sec:method}).

\paragraph{Circular evaluation caveat.}
Structural features (command repetition ratios, file operation counts, edit distances) are computed from the same signals that the labeling functions use to produce silver labels.
Consequently, the in-domain structural F1 of 0.9951 reflects an upper bound on how well these features can \emph{reverse-engineer} the labeling functions, not detection accuracy against true ground truth.
The cross-agent evaluation on OpenHands (0.967 macro-F1) is non-circular: models trained on SWE-Agent LF distributions are tested on a different agent whose LF firing patterns differ substantially (\S\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).
A human-annotated gold set provides the definitive non-circular evaluation (\S\ref{sec:gold_set}).

%-------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We extract sliding windows of $k{=}5$ consecutive actions from agent trajectories.
\textbf{SWE-Agent} trajectories from SWE-bench \citep{jimenez2024swebench,yang2024sweagent} yield 54,809 windows with a stagnation ratio of 28.5\%.
\textbf{OpenHands} (OH) trajectories \citep{wang2025openhands} yield 150,652 windows with a stagnation ratio of 6.6\%.
Silver labels are produced by majority vote over three labeling functions; confidence equals the number of agreeing LFs (1--3).
Unless stated otherwise, evaluation uses the conf${\geq}$2 subset: $N{=}1{,}824$ for SWE-Agent (485 stagnation, 1,339 progress) and $N{=}43{,}376$ for OH.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llrrc}
\toprule
\textbf{Agent} & \textbf{Source} & \textbf{Windows} & \textbf{Stag\,\%} & \textbf{Conf${\geq}$2} \\
\midrule
SWE-Agent & SWE-bench & 54,809 & 28.5 & 15,401 \\
OpenHands & CodeAct   & 150,652 & 6.6 & 43,376 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Dataset statistics. Stagnation ratios differ substantially across agents, reflecting different action distributions.}
\label{tab:dataset_stats}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Detection methods.}
(1)~\textbf{Structural features}: 30 hand-crafted features (repetition rates, file operation counts, edit distances, output entropy) fed to Logistic Regression (LR), Random Forest (RF), and Gradient Boosting (GB).
(2)~\textbf{ProgressProbe}: Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B \citep{zhang2025qwen3embedding} embeddings (1024-dim) processed by a 2-layer GRU \citep{cho2014gru} with attention \citep{bahdanau2015attention} (hidden$=$256, dropout$=$0.1), early-stopped at epoch~3 with patience~5.
(3)~\textbf{Mean-pool + LR}: Mean-pooled Qwen3 embeddings with logistic regression.
(4)~\textbf{LLM-as-Judge}: Claude Sonnet 4.6 zero-shot window-level classification (\$11.66 API cost for the SWE test set).
(5)~\textbf{Cosine similarity}: Cosine distance between consecutive window embeddings with a validation-tuned threshold.

\paragraph{Metrics.}
Macro-F1 is the primary metric. We also report per-class F1 for stagnation (F1\textsubscript{stag}) and progress (F1\textsubscript{prog}).

%-------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Main Results}
\label{sec:main_results}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} presents in-domain results on the SWE-Agent conf${\geq}$2 test set and cross-agent transfer to OpenHands.

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{l ccc cc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{3}{c}{\textbf{SWE-Agent In-Domain} (conf${\geq}$2)} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{OpenHands Transfer}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6}
\textbf{Method} & Macro-F1 & F1\textsubscript{stag} & F1\textsubscript{prog} & All & Conf${\geq}$2 \\
\midrule
Structural RF$^\dagger$       & \textbf{0.9951} & 0.9928 & 0.9974 & 0.9123 & \textbf{0.9959} \\
Structural GB$^\dagger$       & \textbf{0.9951} & 0.9928 & 0.9974 & \textbf{0.9674} & \underline{0.9919} \\
Structural LR$^\dagger$       & 0.9874 & 0.9815 & 0.9933 & 0.7801 & 0.9671 \\
ProgressProbe (GRU)           & \underline{0.9055} & 0.8593 & 0.9518 & 0.530 & 0.528 \\
\quad + full OH fine-tune      & ---    & ---    & ---    & 0.7510 & --- \\
Mean-pool + LR                & 0.8435 & 0.7620 & 0.9250 & ---    & --- \\
LLM-as-Judge                  & 0.6625 & 0.6055 & 0.7195 & ---    & --- \\
Cosine Similarity             & 0.5606 & ---    & ---    & ---    & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Stagnation detection results. In-domain evaluation on SWE-Agent conf${\geq}$2 test set ($N{=}1{,}824$) and zero-shot cross-agent transfer to OpenHands. $^\dagger$Structural metrics are evaluated against LF-derived labels and reflect LF-reconstructibility rather than ground-truth accuracy (see circular evaluation caveat). Best in \textbf{bold}, second-best \underline{underlined}.}
\label{tab:main_results}
\end{table*}

In-domain, structural RF achieves 0.9951 macro-F1, exceeding the GRU probe by 8.96 percentage points (pp) and the LLM judge by 33.3\,pp.
However, the structural--probe gap is inflated by circular evaluation: structural features reconstruct the LFs that generated the labels.
The more informative comparison is among non-circular methods: the GRU probe outperforms mean-pool~+~LR by 6.2\,pp, confirming that temporal modeling within windows adds value beyond static embedding aggregation.
The LLM judge underperforms all learned methods despite substantially higher cost.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Cross-Agent Transfer}
\label{sec:cross_agent}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} (right columns) shows models trained on SWE-Agent and evaluated on OpenHands without any OH training data.
Structural GB transfers best, achieving 0.967 macro-F1 on all OH windows---21.6\,pp above the probe fine-tuned on 100\% of OH data (0.751).
RF drops to 0.912 on all-confidence OH data despite matching GB in-domain, suggesting GB's boosted ensemble is more robust to distributional shift.
Both structural models recover to $>$0.99 on conf${\geq}$2 OH windows, where label noise is lower.

The probe's zero-shot transfer (0.530 on all windows, 0.528 on conf${\geq}$2) is near chance, and even full OH fine-tuning only reaches 0.751.
This asymmetry---structural features transfer naturally while learned embeddings do not---is a central empirical finding: stagnation manifests in action-level structure that generalizes across agents.

\paragraph{Multi-seed stability.}
Structural RF: $0.9951{\pm}0.0000$ in-domain (3 seeds, zero variance); GB cross-agent: $0.9678{\pm}0.0015$.
The probe shows higher variance: $0.9185{\pm}0.0113$, though all seeds exceed 0.90.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Robustness Analysis}
\label{sec:robustness}

\paragraph{Encoder ablation.}
Replacing Qwen3-0.6B with BGE-M3 \citep{chen2024bgem3} or E5-large-v2 \citep{wang2024e5} yields probe macro-F1 of 0.9413 and 0.9363, respectively (vs.\ 0.9055 for Qwen3).
All encoders exceed 0.90, indicating the probe's temporal architecture, not a specific encoder, drives performance. Full results appear in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:encoder_ablation}.

\paragraph{Window size.}
$k{=}5$ is optimal (0.9309 macro-F1); $k{=}3$ drops 5.9\,pp and $k{=}7$ drops 1.8\,pp.
Shorter windows lack sufficient context; longer windows dilute signal by spanning both progress and stagnation phases.
The 0.9309 figure differs from the main table's 0.9055 because the window ablation uses a separate train/val/test split (seed~42, Qwen3-0.6B encoder) to enable controlled comparison across window sizes; cross-split variance accounts for the gap.
Details in Appendix Table~\ref{tab:window_ablation}.

\paragraph{Data scaling.}
Figure~\ref{fig:data_scaling} compares data efficiency across methods.
Structural classifiers are extremely data-efficient: RF achieves 0.991 and GB 0.987 at just 10\% training data, fully saturating by 50\%.
The probe scales monotonically from 0.805 (10\%) to 0.906 (100\%) but has not plateaued---a 10.1\,pp drop that is roughly 20$\times$ the structural degradation (${\sim}$0.4--0.8\,pp).
This data efficiency further supports the structural signal thesis: the features capture patterns so regular that minimal training data suffices.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_data_scaling.pdf}
\caption{Macro-F1 vs.\ training data fraction. Structural classifiers (RF, GB) saturate by 50\% data; the probe scales monotonically but remains 9\,pp below at 100\%.}
\label{fig:data_scaling}
\end{figure}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Labeling Function Temporal Dynamics}
\label{sec:lf_temporal}

We analyze when each LF first fires relative to the onset of stagnation episodes (Table~\ref{tab:lf_temporal}, Figure~\ref{fig:lf_temporal}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{LF} & \textbf{Lag} & \textbf{Lead\,\%} & \textbf{Conc.\,\%} & \textbf{Lag\,\%} \\
\midrule
cmd\_rep      & $-1.87$ & 36.1 & --- & --- \\
file\_diff    & $-1.20$ & 17.1 & --- & --- \\
outcome\_bp   & $+6.70$ & ---  & --- & 48.4 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Temporal roles of labeling functions on SWE-Agent. Lag = mean steps relative to stagnation onset (negative = leading). Kruskal-Wallis $H{=}274.3$, $p{=}2.74{\times}10^{-60}$; all pairwise comparisons significant after Bonferroni correction.}
\label{tab:lf_temporal}
\end{table}

The three LFs occupy distinct temporal niches.
Command repetition leads stagnation onset by 1.87 steps on average, making it an early warning signal.
File diff is near-concurrent ($-1.20$ steps).
Outcome backpropagation lags by 6.70 steps, acting as a retrospective confirmation.

All pairwise differences are significant (Kruskal-Wallis $H{=}274.3$, $p{=}2.74{\times}10^{-60}$; Bonferroni-corrected pairwise: cmd\_rep vs.\ file\_diff $p{=}1.44{\times}10^{-26}$, cmd\_rep vs.\ outcome\_bp $p{=}6.67{\times}10^{-39}$, file\_diff vs.\ outcome\_bp $p{=}3.47{\times}10^{-47}$).

\paragraph{Cross-agent reversal.}
On OpenHands, cmd\_rep shifts from leading ($-1.87$) to lagging ($+3.32$ steps).
This reversal explains the cmd\_rep pathology analyzed in \S\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}: OH's action distribution triggers cmd\_rep on progress windows, inverting its diagnostic value.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_lf_temporal.pdf}
\caption{Temporal roles of labeling functions relative to stagnation onset on SWE-Agent. cmd\_rep leads ($-1.87$ steps), file\_diff is near-concurrent ($-1.20$), outcome\_bp lags ($+6.70$). Inset: OH reversal where cmd\_rep becomes lagging.}
\label{fig:lf_temporal}
\end{figure}

%-------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Command Repetition Ablation}
\label{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}

The cmd\_rep LF votes stagnation on 92.2\% of OH windows (vs.\ 65.5\% on SWE-Agent), with a 71\% false positive rate on OH progress windows.
Table~\ref{tab:cmd_rep_ablation} shows the effect of removing it.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{LF Config} & \textbf{ZS F1} & \textbf{FT F1} & \textbf{FT F1\textsubscript{s}} & \textbf{FT F1\textsubscript{p}} \\
\midrule
3-LF (w/ cmd\_rep) & 0.530 & 0.751 & 0.544 & 0.959 \\
2-LF (no cmd\_rep) & 0.607 & \textbf{0.927} & 0.938 & 0.917 \\
\midrule
$\Delta$ & +7.7\,pp & +17.6\,pp & +39.4\,pp & $-4.2$\,pp \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Effect of removing cmd\_rep on OpenHands probe performance. ZS = zero-shot transfer from SWE-Agent; FT = fine-tuned on 100\% OH data. F1\textsubscript{s}/F1\textsubscript{p} = stagnation/progress F1.}
\label{tab:cmd_rep_ablation}
\end{table}

Removing cmd\_rep improves zero-shot transfer by 7.7\,pp and fine-tuned performance by 17.6\,pp.
The gain is driven almost entirely by stagnation recall (+39.4\,pp F1\textsubscript{stag}), at a modest cost to progress detection ($-4.2$\,pp F1\textsubscript{prog}).
The 3-LF fine-tuned model's stagnation F1 of 0.544 reveals that cmd\_rep corrupts silver labels so thoroughly that even full fine-tuning on OH data cannot recover.
Note that removing cmd\_rep also reduces training data from 101,713 to 72,554 windows (28.5\% reduction); however, the model trained on \emph{less} data with cleaner labels decisively outperforms the model trained on \emph{more} data with corrupted labels, indicating that label quality rather than data volume drives the improvement.
This motivates agent-specific LF selection: labeling functions validated on one agent architecture may become adversarial on another.
Appendix Table~\ref{tab:cross_agent_cmdrep} extends this analysis to structural zero-shot transfer, where removing cmd\_rep has the opposite effect ($-4.2$\,pp), revealing that the fix requires learned adaptation rather than simple LF deletion.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Temporal Label Models}
\label{sec:temporal_label_models}

We investigate whether incorporating LF temporal dynamics into label aggregation improves over majority vote (MV).
Table~\ref{tab:temporal_models} compares three temporal label models \citep{ratner2017snorkel,safranchik2020linkedhmm}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Conf${\geq}$2 F1} & \textbf{MV Agr.} & \textbf{Probe F1$^\ddagger$} \\
\midrule
MV (baseline) & 0.8946$^\ast$ & 1.000 & 0.8468 \\
TWV ($\sigma{=}2$) & \textbf{0.9003} & 0.885 & 0.8211 \\
Lag-HMM & 0.8936 & 0.820 & --- \\
HMM & 0.7502 & 0.685 & 0.6564 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Temporal label model comparison. $^\ast$MV conf${\geq}$2 F1 is a self-evaluation baseline (MV evaluated against itself on high-confidence windows). $^\ddagger$Downstream: probe trained on each method's labels, evaluated on the full SWE-Agent test set ($N{=}8{,}101$) against MV labels.}
\label{tab:temporal_models}
\end{table}

TWV achieves the highest conf${\geq}$2 F1 (0.9003, +0.57\,pp over MV), but this gain does not propagate downstream: a probe trained on TWV labels scores 0.8211 vs.\ 0.8468 for MV-trained ($-2.57$\,pp).
TWV flips 14.1\% of conf$=$1 labels, introducing noise that hurts probe training.
HMM overpredicts stagnation (29,176 stagnation vs.\ 25,633 progress windows), degrading downstream performance to 0.6564.
Lag-HMM closely tracks MV ($-0.1$\,pp) despite explicitly modeling temporal offsets, suggesting that the temporal structure captured by individual LFs does not improve \emph{aggregation}.

\paragraph{TWV sensitivity.}
The Gaussian kernel bandwidth $\sigma$ is critical: $\sigma{=}2$ is optimal (0.9003); $\sigma{=}1$ drops to 0.8105 and $\sigma{=}5$ drops to 0.808.
This narrow operating range, combined with the downstream degradation, makes TWV impractical as a drop-in MV replacement.

These results constitute a negative finding: despite clear temporal structure in LF firing patterns (\S\ref{sec:lf_temporal}), temporal label models do not improve over simple majority vote for downstream stagnation detection.

%-------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Gold Set Validation}
\label{sec:gold_set}
\goldset{Human-annotated gold set (650 windows) validation pending. Results will be added upon completion (expected 2026-05-17). This provides the non-circular ground truth evaluation for all methods.}

