These results support a nuanced conclusion: LF temporal dynamics have diagnostic value for understanding cross-agent failure modes, but cannot be directly operationalized as zero-shot improvements---the fix requires agent-specific fine-tuning.

\subsubsection{Labeling Functions}
\label{sec:labeling_functions}

We design three LFs that target complementary signals of stagnation (Table~\ref{tab:lf_design}).

\paragraph{Command repetition ($\lambda_1$).}
Computes the proportion of repeated commands within window $w_i$.
A high repetition ratio triggers a stagnation vote, capturing overt repetitive behavior such as re-running failing commands.
This LF never abstains: it votes stagnation when the repetition ratio exceeds a threshold and progress otherwise.
It votes stagnation on 65.5\% of SWE-Agent windows and 92.2\% of OpenHands windows; the elevated OH rate reflects differences in action distributions across agent architectures (\S\ref{sec:cmd_rep_ablation}).

\paragraph{File diff delta ($\lambda_2$).}
Measures the fraction of steps within $w_i$ that produce code modifications (file creates, edits, or deletes).
A low modification frequency triggers a stagnation vote; windows with more than one edited file receive a progress vote; windows with exactly one or zero edits abstain ($\bot$).
On OpenHands, this LF achieves 87\% correctness on progress windows, making it the most reliable cross-agent signal.

\paragraph{Outcome backpropagation ($\lambda_3$).}
Uses the final task outcome (resolved/unresolved) as distant supervision.
For unresolved tasks, the last two windows and any windows following a timeout or stuck signal receive stagnation votes; resolved tasks receive progress votes in the corresponding windows.
All other windows abstain ($\bot$).
Unlike $\lambda_1$ and $\lambda_2$, which operate on local window content, $\lambda_3$ propagates a global signal backward through time.
This makes it a form of distant supervision: individually noisy, but complementary to the real-time signals when aggregated.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{llcc}
\toprule
\textbf{LF} & \textbf{Signal} & \textbf{SWE Stag\,\%} & \textbf{OH Stag\,\%} \\
\midrule
$\lambda_1$ (cmd\_rep)    & Repetition & 65.5 & 92.2 \\
$\lambda_2$ (file\_diff)  & Output     & 57.3 & 42.1 \\
$\lambda_3$ (outcome\_bp) & Outcome    & 23.9 & 19.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Labeling function design. Stag\,\% = fraction of windows receiving a stagnation vote from the LF (remaining windows receive progress or abstain). SWE = SWE-Agent; OH = OpenHands.}
\label{tab:lf_design}
\end{table}

